1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
The record says: delivered on time, in full. What actually happened: one pallet line held the wrong pack size.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,300
Monday 22 June, 6:12 am. The warehouse truck reaches Store 014 inside its 06:00–06:30 slot.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:15,700
At 03:00 the picker scanned the 5 kg slot’s label, then took 16 bales from the slot next door: 1 kg packs.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:21,100
The trip log: left at 04:30, arrived at 06:12. On time.

5
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:27,500
The clerk scans the pallet label and the notice matches. 64 bags of 5 kg are booked. They do not exist.

6
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:33,400
The OTIF scorecard: 88% for June. The 22 June delivery counts as perfect.

7
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:39,800
Friday’s count: 5 kg −63 bags, 1 kg +157 packs. Theft? Sloppy counting?

8
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:46,700
What actually happened: the 5 kg shelf stood empty for 51.5 open hours while the books showed 63.

9
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:53,100
Four corrections in two buildings, one mis-pick. Across the company they net to ₹80.

10
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:59,500
Weigh every pallet: the mistake is caught at dispatch. The shelf stays full, and OTIF falls to 84%.

11
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,500
Every record agreed. That is exactly why nobody checked the pallet.
